ht costs were  going up by about four percent  a year that was already really high  double the two percent you'd normally  get that's the bank of england's target  but just look at how that bar's gone up  today we'll add a red one on showing you  today's bar  wow it is up by basically more than  double uh or almost double i should say  uh but that that is you know an enormous  increase it's the biggest since the  creation of the obr we're going to see  the highest level inflation that we've  seen going back generations and that all  goes into our pockets and that's the  issue that the chancellor is facing is  how can he deal with that and what kind  of policies can he introduce that will  actually make that a little bit better  and the question there when it comes to  those policies and just how much of a  difference they're going to make is  what kind of impact are they going to  have here you can see it in fiscal terms  this is just the amount of money that  either is kind of going into the  exchange or coming out anything below  the bar shows that he's spending money  to try to provide extra cash for people  and you can see that in this year that  we're in at the moment the one that  we're going into that's your fuel duty  that's national insurance kicking in  there and then there's smaller giveaways  in the future future years but as i was  saying you know it's nothing to  compensate for what we saw uh in you  know for what we're experiencing this  year the biggest fall in our standard of  living that we have really ever seen uh  going all the way back to 1955. so yes  sir jane it is it is grim i'm afraid and  you know the only kind of reassuring  news is that were it not for some of the  you know the policies we've seen for the  chancellor things might have been a  little bit worse but frankly  he could have potentially done more so  some big questions remaining for the  chancellor and indeed uh we will see  what he says and how he justifies this  and we'll als